.el .ds Aq '
.TH b4-export 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-export \- Export tables to files, one file per table
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 export\fR [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-table\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIDIR\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Export tables to files, one file per table
.PP
Write the tables of the database into DIR, one file per table, named after the table (bookings.csv, accounts.csv, ...). DIR is created if it does not exist; existing files of the same name are replaced.
.PP
The files hold the exact values. The format is chosen with \-\-format, or with the global \-\-output when that is csv or jsonl. A table whose name cannot be a file name on every system (a path separator, a colon, a device name of Windows such as NUL) is not exported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
The file format [default: csv]

Without \-\-format, the global \-\-output selects the format when it is csv or jsonl. `\-\-output table` and `\-\-output json` are errors, and so is an \-\-output that contradicts \-\-format: export writes csv and jsonl only.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-table\fR \fI<TABLE>\fR
Export only this table; repeat for several tables
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIDIR\fR>
The directory to write the files into
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 export ./export
    Write every table as CSV into ./export.
b4 export ./export \-\-format jsonl \-\-table bookings \-\-table accounts
    Write two tables as JSON lines.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1)
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
